answers:
## Step 1: Analyzing the given system of equations
The system of equations provided is:
\begin{align*}
xy + 4z &= 60 \\
yz + 4x &= 60 \\
zx + 4y &= 60.
\end{align*}
We need to find the set $S$ of possible values of $x$ and then calculate the sum of the squares of the elements of $S$.

## Step 2: Subtracting the equations to eliminate variables
By subtracting the equations from each other, we can eliminate variables. Subtracting the first equation from the second gives:
\begin{align*}
yz + 4x - (xy + 4z) &= 60 - 60 \\
yz - xy + 4x - 4z &= 0 \\
y(z - x) - 4(z - x) &= 0 \\
(z - x)(y - 4) &= 0.
\end{align*}
This implies either $z = x$ or $y = 4$.

## Step 3: Considering the case when $y = 4$
If $y = 4$, substituting back into the first equation gives:
\begin{align*}
4x + 4z &= 60 \\
x + z &= 15.
\end{align*}
Using the second equation with $y = 4$:
\begin{align*}
4z + 4x &= 60 \\
z + x &= 15.
\end{align*}
This does not provide new information, so we look at the third equation:
\begin{align*}
zx + 4y &= 60 \\
zx + 16 &= 60 \\
zx &= 44.
\end{align*}
Now we have a system of equations in $x$ and $z$:
\begin{align*}
x + z &= 15 \\
xz &= 44.
\end{align*}
Solving this system for $x$ and $z$ gives:
\begin{align*}
z &= \frac{44}{x} \\
x + \frac{44}{x} &= 15 \\
x^2 - 15x + 44 &= 0 \\
(x - 11)(x - 4) &= 0.
\end{align*}
So, $x = 11$ or $x = 4$ when $y = 4$.

